      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2766.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 684,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance on Earth, uniquely suited to support life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is famous for being delightfully \"weird\" because its chemical structure gives it unique physical properties:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or vapor).\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its molecular structure (it has a slight positive charge on one side and a slight negative charge on the other), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals in the earth.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 2. Earth’s Most Abundant Resource\n* **The \"Blue Planet\":** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which account for about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarce Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh water. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes, forms clouds in the atmosphere, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. The Essence of Life\n* **Biological Necessity:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. \n* **Cellular Function:** Water acts as the internal environment in which all the cellular processes of life take place. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports oxygen and nutrients through the bloodstream.\n* **Habitability:** Astrobiologists searching for life on other planets always look for one primary ingredient first: **liquid water**. \n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our geography, driving our weather, and making the existence of biological life possible."
